triumph of right-wing populists in Austria, overall they come to 29 percent
A noticeable weakening of both parties of the previous grand coalition and a significant strengthening of the right-wing populist parties brought after the first preliminary results the early parliamentary elections of Sunday in Austria.
The Austrian People's Party (ÖVP) suffered particularly severe losses, where the position of the former Chairman Wilhelm Molterer is seriously on the edge.
In contrast, the electoral director of the Alliance for the Future of Austria (BZÖ), the Carinthian Governor Jörg Haider, is gearing up for a triumphant return to federal politics.
According to the previous results, the Social Democracy (SPÖ) with 29.8 percent of the vote remains the strongest force in the country, compared to the last elections in 2006, it loses 5.5 percent of the vote.
The ÖVP scored even worse, losing almost nine percent of the vote at 25.6 percent.
These are the worst results of these two major parties in post-war Austrian history, and especially among the People's Party, which had demanded the early elections, this is a really disastrous result.
At the beginning of July, when the ÖVP had withdrawn from the coalition, the People's Party had a clear lead over the SPÖ.
Voters, however, have probably punished the party for letting the government fail.
Already in the first reactions to the results there was speculation about a possible resignation of the party's chairman and previous vice-chancellor Wilhelm Molterer.
Observers believe that this could already be the case on Monday at an extraordinary meeting of the party's board.
Such a development would probably pave the way to the most likely way out of the election debacle, i.e. a renewal of the grand coalition of the SPÖ and ÖVP.
In view of the strengthening of both right-wing populist parties - the Freiheitliche (FPÖ) provisionally received 18 percent, the BZÖ eleven percent of the votes - but at the same time expressed their fear that the year 1999 could be repeated when the People's Party had agreed with the populists (at that time the FPÖ was still uniform, in 2005 it split from the BZÖ) with regard to a common government, which had even imposed sanctions on the part.
Jörg Haider, who already declared that he is ready to work with every party and assumes a return to Vienna in Austrian federal politics, is obviously counting on this development.
In addition, the party of the Greens moved into parliament, which, however, suffered slight losses and slipped from third to fifth place among the Austrian political parties.
No other party has probably mastered the four percent hurdle and therefore did not move into parliament.
Schwarzenberg: Relations with India may cool down
If it fails to solve the problem with the Czech scientists who have been indicted in India for unlawful collecting beetles, according to Czech Foreign Minister Karel Schwarzenberg, there could be a some slowdown in Czech-Indian relations.
This was emphasized by the minister in New York after today's meeting with his Indian counterpart Pranab Mukherdshi.
"If this does not clear up, then for sure, then there will be some cooling," he explained after a meeting that took place on a Czech initiative.
Mukherdshi said after the talks that it was difficult for the executive to intervene in judicial matters.
In spite of everything, he argued that that in the case of Petr Švácha, who was acquitted by the indictment and received a fine, wanted the central government with the local government in Dardjiling, in whose jurisprudence the matter falls.
"His problem could be clarified," the minister said.
For Emil Kučera, who was sentenced to three years imprisonment, Mukherdshi's view it would be the best if he were to appeal with a higher authority.
He could thus request release on bail and wait in freedom for his verdict, according to the minister.
Schwarzenberg confirmed that his partner had also commented on negotiations.
"He recommends an appeal to a higher court, then a pardon would be possible for him," said the head of Czech diplomacy.
The only way the Czech side can intervene in the whole matter is to allow the convicted to "competent legal support," the Indian minister said.
At the same time, he said that such assistance would already be offered them by the Indian side.
“We have a keen interest in maintaining good contacts with India that have a long-term tradition.
The Indians also admit that," Schwarzenberg said.
At the same time, according to the Czech press agency ČTK, he reaffirmed the Czech Republic’s attention to the problem of Czech entomologists.
"We really consider it a big mistake to imprison and accuse these Czech scientists," he explained.
An answer to the question of whether he had received a commitment from the Indian side to deal with the matter, the minister evaded.
"I've already been promised quite a lot," he merely stated.
A Czech judges the wrestling around the bottom of the sea
He approaches an electronic map depicting Australia and the adjacent sea areas.
However, he is not interested in the continent, but a small dot, lost in the blue of the ocean.
"The island belongs to Australia and, together with it, the adjacent seabed.
The area is as large as the continent itself," says Italian lawyer Luigi Santosuosso, who is one of the thirty-member staff of the U.N. Divison for Oceans and Maritime Rights.
Its boss, the division director, the Czech lawyer V�áclav Mikulka, pursues the interpretation and complements it.
This is where work and enthusiasm prevail.
Until now, the bottom of the sea has a little-known world rich in natural springs.
Oil can be found here, furthermore ore and other non-renewable raw materials, but also hitherto unknown living organisms, which, for example, carry the key to the development of a drug against cancer.
A seabed, larger than the mainland
Today, the neighbouring states vehemently filed their entitlement to the new sea areas.
According to a UN convention, they make use of their rights, on which they agreed in 1982 in Montego Bay in Jamaica.
It is mainly about the area of the so-called continental shelf, which often extends to a few hundred kilometers far from the shore of a country into the sea.
According to the Convention, this country has an exclusive entitlement to all sources of the continental shelf.
“Simply speaking, as a continental shelf is the part of the seabed and its soles, which represents a natural sequel to the continent below the sea surface.
It crosses the boundaries of the shore, often the boundary of two hundred nautical miles of the so-called exclusive economic zone," explains Mikulka.
The application submitted by the relevant country to the Commission to fix the external borders of the continental shelf must be substantiated by extensive scientific and technical documentation based on many cost-intensive investigations and measurements.
It is the result of many years of preparatory work.
“One of our tasks is to provide technical support to the Commission on a legal and technical basis.
The Commission has so far negotiated over five applications from Russia, Brazil, Australia, Ireland and New Zealand.
Until now, only the latter three countries have succeeded," Mikulka adds.
Australia's success was recently met with an international response, which became the first country to significantly increase its maritime areas.
Yet New Zealand is also a winner - the expansion of the seabed, which was granted to the country last week by the commission, is about 1.6 million square kilometres, about six times more than the size of the actual state.
Both countries invested millions of dollars in the investigations.
The gain that flows to them in the future from the use of the seabed will, as they promise, have invaluable value.
Other states will also gradually submit their applications.
They can do this for ten years from the moment they ratify the "Montego Bay Agreement."
Where the continental shelf ends, the territory of the ocean floor begins, described by the convention as a "common heritage of humanity."
Here, then, no state holds a special position.
The right to promote in this field is granted by an international institution established by the Convention.
"However, the seabed is not a gold-digging country," says Mikulka, adding its "individual areas are legally well defined and the system enshrined contractually."
Czech
Part of the seabed also belongs to the Czech Republic today.
It shares it with other states of the former Eastern bloc within the framework of the joint organization "Interoceanmetal".
In 1991, due to the convention, as well as the investors of a few other states, this received the status of a so-called "groundbreaking investor".
The organization has the right to promoter in the ClarionClipperton zone in the Silent Ocean between Mexico, Hawai and Kiribati.
The area to be investigated by the investor is slightly larger than former Czechoslovakia.
The seabed is divided there, it is a kind of tatra on the seabed.
“You can promote there on a total area of 75,000 square kilometers.
The area is a rich site of polymetal concretions containing nickel, copper, cobalt and manganese," according to another lawyer, the Indian Pakshi Rajan.
The Macedonian collaborator of the Rober Sandev division brings such concretion as a pattern.
It is reminiscent of a small potato or, with some imagination, of the fabricent Schem, which has brought to live according to the Golem.
German special forces were on the scene at hostage liberation
The Egyptian government had requested help from Germany to solve the hostage crisis - the experts arrived.
When it became serious, the special forces were ready, but they were not used.
More and more details are becoming known about the circumstances of the liberation of eleven European tourists kidnapped in Egypt.
According to the Ministry of Defence and the Ministry of the Interior, German special forces were also indirectly involved in the rescue operation.
Accordingly, specialists of the Bundeswehr as well as officials of the GSG 9, the aviation squadron of the federal police, the Federal Criminal Police Office as well as logistics experts of the Technical Relief Agency were at the place of liberation.
However, according to spokesmen for the ministries on Monday evening, the German forces were not needed because the hostage-takers let their prisoners run and fled when they recognized signs of violent liberation.
The Egyptian government has accepted the assistance offered by Germany in order to involve it as support forces on the ground.
"Thankfully, it didn't come to that," said a spokesman for the Federal Ministry of the Interior.
Attackers had kidnapped the tour group of the five Germans, five Italians, one Romanian and their eight Egyptian companions on September 19 in southern Egypt.
After kidnapping in a desert area in southern Egypt, the hostages had been deported back and forth in the border area between Egypt, Libya, Sudan and Chad.
The identity of the hostage takers remained unclear until recently, as did the details of the exemption or possible ransom payments.
Those rescued have since arrived safely in Cairo.
Steinmeier said the freed hostages were in the care of the German embassy.
He did not comment on details of the release.
There was initially no information in Berlin on when they returned to Germany.
According to "Bild" newspaper, the five holidaymakers and the special forces are to be flown out with an Airbus of the Luftwaffe and arrive in Germany on Tuesday.
U.S. House of Representatives rejects bailout
The first chamber of the U.S. Congress has narrowly failed the €700 billion bailout for the financial markets.
The Dow Jones rushed into the depths.
By 206 votes to 227, the U.S. House of Representatives has rejected the government's bailout for financial markets.
Despite all the appeals from U.S. President George W. Bush and leaders of both parties, there was not a majority for the program to curb the chaos in financial markets.
In a dramatic action, the proponents had desperately tried to convince the critics of the $700 billion package.
Barney Frank, one of Democratic negotiators, had called MPs for approval even if the plan was unpopular.
"It's hard to get political recognition for avoiding something that hasn't even kicked in yet," he acknowledged.
Still over the course of voting, the Dow Jones slumped by more than six percent, or by 600 points.
The reason was investors' worries, the government bailout could fail.
He recovered shortly before the end of the vote, then, after the failure became known.
Experts are now expecting further severe turmoil in international financial markets.
For the entry into force of the program, the approval of both the House of Representatives and the Senate is necessary.
Organizers record rising booking figures
It seems to become a good winter for German tour operators:
According to a study, sales for travel bookings for the coming months have increased significantly compared to the previous year – even by more than 30 percent for the winter season.
Despite weak economic prospects, the travel appetite of Germans remains unclouded according to a study.
When booking for the winter season, the rice bureaus recorded an increase in sales of 31 percent compared to the same period of the previous year, as the German Travel Association (DRV) announced on Monday in Berlin.
According to the study by the market research institute GfK, there are already increased booking figures for the autumn months.
For example, the increase in sales of the travel agencies for October amounted to around eleven percent compared to 2007, the DRV said.
The basis for these results are figures from GfK, which evaluates monthly booking data from 1200 German travel agencies and calculates them for the overall market.
The travel bookings made until the end of August 2008 are recorded.
Due to the weak dollar price, travel to the USA in particular is in demand, the DRV said.
Here, the booking increase amounts to around a third.
In addition, Spain, Turkey and Greece as well as the domestic areas are particularly sought-after destinations among Germans.
In particular, package holidays would be very popular, explained DRV President Klaus Laepple.
In view of the increasing costs of energy and food, the costs “for the customer can be calculated better than for the individually organized holiday”.
Consumers could save money, among other things, through early bird discounts or all-inclusive offers, Laepple explained.
The summer months were also very well booked, so that there would again be an increase in sales compared to the previous year - although, according to a further study, the number of Germans taking a trip fell compared to the previous year.
My first time Oktoberfest
Ballermann-Apres-Ski-Hits in the endless loop, beer from liter jugs and celebrities in designer dirndls.
It's a Wiesn debut.
Susanne Kronzucker and I don’t really have much in common.
She's blonde, TV presenter and wears a bespoke dirndl.
I have dark hair, I'm a student and my dress is secondhand.
One thing, however, unites us: Barely three minutes on the Wiesn, the photographers pounce on us and snap the memory cards full - very flattering and that on my first Wiesn visit.
That's how you make friends.
The cheap dirndl seems to fulfill its purpose.
As Wiesn-Touri and actually located north of the Weißwurstequator, the feeling of well-being in the costume was still limited until now.
But once arrived at Theresienwiese, everything is half as bad.
Almost everyone wears Dirndl or Lederhosn; none looks disgusted or embarrassed - all completely normal.
Weird glances at the chocolate banana stand only reaps my dirndl apron.
A Munich woman alerts me to the fact that I would have tied the apron incorrectly: "Left hoast free, middle virgin, on the right assigned, widowed at the back."
Secretly, I freed the loop from back to left.
"Fit," it said, laughing.
Others make less effort about subtle flirting techniques and wear simple felt hats, on which "To drunk to get dressed!" stands and stretch the bare butt against an uninvited.
The young man with the exhibitionist attitudes is called Bob, comes from England and also visits the Oktoberfest for the first time.
He finds it "gorgeous," with a long drawn "ooooo."
That's what he tells me after getting rid of his previously drunk measure.
Right next to one of those hydrants that everyone thinks he's a trash can.
Bob is one of those notorious "wild bieslers."
Others aren't lucky enough to get rid of the alcohol in this way.
Around eight o'clock in the evening, paramedics walk in droves across the fairground, who have to take care of the poor swallowers.
The nurses always appear in the pack - always four at a time a covered stretcher from tent to tent.
This works as with most Wiesn visitors: Empty, fully back again.
You don't see who's under the tarpaulin in the Promille coffin.
"That's what everyone's packing," one of the paramedics shouted to me on request and vanished in the turmoil.
name of accident victim announced by the police
The name of a 63-year-old female pedestrian who died in the hospital after being hit by a vehicle in Essex has been announced.
Carole Paulson, residing at Chelmsford Road in Brentwood, was involved in Friday afternoon's accident at Shenfield Road in the city centre.
She was taken to Queen's hospital in Romford but later died.
A 51-year-old man was arrested on suspicion of reckless driving after the accident.
He has since been released on bail.
Turkey expands diplomatic horizons
Long before Turkey aimed to join the European Union, European powers were interested in going deep into Turkey's hinterland.
On the eastern side of the Bosphorus, perhaps just 20 steps within Asia, stands one of the finest relics of this failed, imperial aspiration.
Hydarpasha station was designed by the two German architects Otto Ritter and Helmut Conu in a neo-Renaissance style.
That's at least what the travel guides are telling you.
The building sits at the end of the platforms with a tower at every corner of the front and thus looks like some provincial German town hall.
The rock is dirty.
The whole building disintegrates
But once you're inside the vaulted ticket counter, there's no doubt about the sublimity of the project whose westernmost approach this was.
The station was opened in August 1908 by a German Consortium, the Anatolia-Baghdad Society.
Germany, which was a precipitator in the imperialist game, had to implement its ambitions to dominate the Middle East by means of a train connection to Damascus, Baghdad, and further out to what Saudi Arabia is today.
Yet Germany's imperialist projects slammed during the First World War.
His railroad tracks were attacked by Lawrence of Arabia and his Arabs.
The relative isolation from its surrounding region, generated by the insurmountable frontiers of the Cold War, has disappeared.
British forces withdrew from Egypt and pushed the Turks and their German allies from Palestine and Syria
And there was also bad news for Hyderpasha station.
In 1917, an ammunition train flew into the air, destroying the ornate roof.
Turkish sources say it was sabotage.
Since then, it has slowly disintegrated, largely overlooked by history.
Nowadays, however, this railway line serves, at least symbolically, as a gateway to Anatolia and everything east of it, to highlight the expansion of Turkey's diplomatic horizon.
The ruling Judiciary and Development Party, the AKP, has introduced a completely new foreign policy for the country.
Relative isolation from the neighboring regions, generated by the Cold War limits, has disappeared.
There is now a policy of rapprochement.
Remarkably, the Turkish government has good relations with Israel and Syria, the Palestinian splinter groups Hamas and Fatah, Iran and Iraq and, of course, with the European Union and the United States.
In order to be able to assess the key data of this new foreign policy, I have boardeded the overnight train to the Turkish capital Ankara to visit one of the AKP's leading foreign policy experts, the Turkish MP Suat Kiniklioglu.
The next morning, about ten and a half hours later, after a bumpy ride on tracks that produced the lockstep of the old world in the well-known documentary "The Night Mail", I arrived in the Turkish capital, which is regarded as a new city, for the new secular state project to the Ottoman past.
The AKP, however, which is known for its Islamist roots, represents a paradigm shift in Turkish unity
This has sparked some suspicion in the West, especially in Washington.
A taxi has taken me to the AKP's headquarters, a custom-made, ultra-modern stone and steel structure, in a developing suburb, surrounded by half-finished shopping malls and pubs where middle managers in suits, both men and women, met to complete the first stores of the day.
Suat Kiniklioglu could be a centre-right politician from almost any other European political party.
Suit, tie, I'm sure he's even packed a Blackberry.
“The goal of the party’s foreign policy is now to reintegrate Turkey with its immediate neighbouring countries.
The Cold War was an exceptional phenomenon," he tells me.
Turkey had been cut off from its hinterland.
These days she has to look east and west at the same time.
There wouldn't be either one or the other.
She'd have to do both
"But certainly Turkey could eventually," I asked, "being forced to decide?"
"Turkey," he said, "didn't want to have to face this decision."
He then emphasized a point that almost everyone with whom I spoke here almost routinely said: Turkey’s relations, not only with the Middle East, but also with the Caucasus and around the Black Sea, are just as beneficial for Europe and the United States as they are for Turkey.
With Turkey on board, it seems to be the embassy figuratively, Europe as a whole is heading east towards Asia and the Middle East.
Back at Hydarpasha's station, after another largely sleepless night, I thought of these two German rulers Herr Ritter and Mr. Conu, who had been watching the construction of this building 100 years ago.
How surprised they would have been to learn that Europe's ambitions in the Middle East could now, to a large extent, be in the hands of Turkey.
And when I waited at the dock for the ferry that was supposed to take me back across the Bosphorus, I remembered that Turkey was always called "the sick man of Europe" in those days.
At that time, at least, there didn't seem much doubt about which continent should be entitled to this fascinating country.
Commemorating Vernon Handley and William Woodruff.
Mark Brown writes: As Guildford's musical director since 1962, Vernon "Tod" Handley (obituary on September 11) was in charge of the Guildford Philharmonic and Choir.
As part of his music program for the city, he founded his great youth choir, Proteus, the following year.
It was intended for teenagers up to 21 years, but when the choir sang for the last time in 1979, some of us were already at the end of 20 - we simply refused to leave the choir.
The name "Proteus" (the mythological shape changer) stood for the choir's readiness and ability to tackle any piece of music presented to it, whether it was a madrigal or from the Messiah, whether a capella or with a full orchestra.
The musical versatility of such a young choir was often astonishing, always thrilling and consisted solely thanks to the inspiration of that man and his enthusiasm for the music, which never subsided for years.
How often death kept us to "let the music speak for itself."
He used to say, "The composer knows what he wants."
It's all in music.
So it's singing!"
His style of conducting was absolutely clear and precise, never bossy but never exuberant.
However high the musical level was, he always demanded an even higher.
John Frayn Turner writes:
If any conductor should have been knighted, it was Vernon Handley.
I knew him well during the last two decades, from 1962 to 1982, during which he led the Guildford Philharmonic.
Over the last 40 years, I’ve written reviews for more than a hundred of his concerts and I can sincerely say I’ve never been disappointed by a single one—often I’ve been inspired by them.
Tod Handley was that rare combination of greatness and personal humility.
He loathed conductors who, according to his words, simply "peddling with one or two well-known chaunt horses in the world."
So many of us will miss him.
William Woodruff
Professor John Beckett writes:
In your obituary for William Woodruff (September 25) you write that "he resumed teaching economic history at Oxford in 1946 and received a Houblon-Norman research fellowship in 1950 ... while at the same time he was studying for a BSc, an MA and a doctoral degree.
This is a conclusion from an author's note in Woodruff's book "Beyond Nab End," but it isn't true.
From 1946 to 1953 he was a lecturer in economic history at the University of Nottingham and during this time he wrote a doctoral thesis on the British rubber industry under the supervision of Professor J.D. Chambers, which was published in 1958 as "The British Rubber Industry".
He was in the United States from 1950-1951 in the course of a Houblon-Norman Scholarship, and spent the summer of 1952 at the Universities of the Universities of Illinois, Harvard and New York.
In 1953 he finally left Nottingham and went to Illinois.
It's not known why Woodruff wanted to cover up that part of his career.
J. Vicente Resino at the Faculty of Philology.
`I commend the exhibition of José Vicente Resino `Flamenco and life' because his photographs in black-and-wheild, in documentary style, are fantastic.
They made me aware of them because of their simplicity and because of the power they radiate, how they always deal with the protagonists with respect.
An exhibition that shows a lot of feelings.
Resino knows how to find the magical moments.”
second round of excellent sales at Honda.
In November, Honda catches marketing the second-generation Honda Jazz.
While its predecessor model had a large interior, this wide-body sedan offers a much larger one.
In addition, there are also the technical subtleties, which allow even more comfortable driving and gasoline savings.
changes before the imminent revolution.
This is the second generation of Honda Jazz.
Although, aesthetically speaking, the new jazz has a similar line to its predecessor, the changes that have been made in this large sedan are numerous.
Among other things, the innovative design, the new room flexibility, the larger dimensions of the car and the new mechanics are to be emphasized.
Nevertheless, before starting to analyze the new model, it must be said that this new jazz appears six years later, after the first generation appeared on the market.
According to the first observations, Honda managed to renew this model for a shorter time compared to the other manufacturers.
Although the Honda Jazz is less well-known in Europe, this model is a phenomenon as far as its sale in the other markets is concerned.
That's a case in Japan, in the country where jazz is the best-selling car of the year 2002 and also later this 2008.
After the jazz had appeared on the market, about a million models were sold in Japan alone.
The second sales market was the Old Continent, with about half a million cars sold in six years.
Even so, Honda is trying to increase the sale of the new jazz model within the segment.
In this way, while Japanese company sold a total of 83 000 models of Jazz in 2007, the expected estimates amount to 10 0000 cars, which is 17% of the assumed growth on the European market.
As for the Spanish market, Honda has sold a total of 8 000 Jazz models since 2002, 6.7% of the total sale at Honda in Spain.
As far as the new generation of the model is concerned, the managers of the branch in Spain expect to be able to sell 2 000 jazz models per year.
More precisely, the appearance of the new Honda Jazz model allows to cover up the sales reduction in Spain.
In reality, the heads of the brand hope to conclude the year with a total sale of 22 000 models, which is 4 000 fewer automobiles than in 2007, a reduction of 16%.
Despite this increase in sales, Honda has increased its market share in Spain year-on-year (1.6%) to 1.8%.
At the same time, the Japanese company hopes to reach the 2% in 2010.
To achieve this proportion, Honda relies on the appearance of a new model.
It’s Insight, a 5-door hybrid car with a compact size of 4.7 meters in length, the Japanese company hopes to sell about 200 000 models per year all over the world.
According to the Japanese manufacturer’s market estimates, 50% of Insight models are sold in the United States, while the restant share is distributed on the Old Continent and Japan.
The appearance of insight on the market is planned for the first months of the year, for the first time its public presentation will take place in January in Valencia.
With a view to the first generation, the new jazz grows in all its sizes.
Its length is 3.90 meters (plus 5 centimeters), its width is 1.69 meters (plus 2 centimeters) and the distance between the axles is 2.50 meters, this leads to the assumption that it has become 5 centimeters larger.
The height, on the other hand, does not change and remains 1.52 meters.
Together with increasing the dimensions, the interior is also enlarged.
This success is due, on the one hand, to the new spatial flexibility program and, on the other hand, to the central position of the fuel tank, a solution that was also used in Jazz's predecessor model.
The capacity of the trunk, which has a system of the double loading area, also reaches up to 339 liters, i.e. 19 liters larger than the capacity of the trunk of the first generation.
It must be emphasized that these measures are available in the version with a 1.4 liter petrol engine, because the capacity of the trunk of the Honda Jazz with a 1.2-liter petrol engine is 379 liters.
Among other improvements, in addition to the additional equipment elements such as leather seats or the possibility of installing panoramic windows, improved access to the seats is offered.
The opening angle of the backdoors is 80 degrees, with the first jazz having only 67 degrees.
Jazz's second-generation engines are also new.
At the beginning of its marketing, this small model has 2 versions of the petrol engines: 1.2 and 1.4 liters.
Both variants have i-VTEC system, which enables low fuel consumption and lower CO2 emissions, has excellent technical performance and at the same time provides high-torque performance for the entire engine runtime.
The first version with 1.2 liters achieves the output of 90 hp at 6 000 rotations with maximum torque of 114 Nm at 4 900 min?1 .
This petrol engine - Jazz's predecessor reached 78 horsepower - has 5.3 liters of petrol consumption and CO2 emissions of 125 grams.
In view of the previous engine, which reached 83 hp in the previous generation, it now manages to reach 100 hp.
The Jazz 100 horsepower 1.4 has 5.4 liters of petrol consumption and 128 grams of CO2 emissions.
`Car bee' avoiding collisions
To advance the development of technologies for accident prevention, this time Nissan was inspired by nature, more precisely, by the bees.
The Japanese company has developed a robot whose flight stems from the swarm behavior of bees and avoids accidents.
This robot is a Bio-mimetic Car Robot Drive or BR23C and represents the first step in accident prevention technologies.
The engineers from Nissan Motor Co.'s technical development center developed this small robot in cooperation with Research Center for Advanced Science and Technology at the prestigious University of Tokyo (Japan).
Kazuhiro Doi, head of technology PR and R&D brand management, the BR23C represents an extension of the four-layer Nissan safety shield.
This robot is intended to ensure that this brand's cars are able to avoid the collisions.
During the flight, each bee builds its own, oval-shaped aerial space very similar to the Nissan safety shield.
But the most important are the faceted eyes of the bee, which can see in all directions over an angle of 300 degrees, this allows you to fly undisturbed in its personal airspace without interruption.
In order to replicate the all-round perspective, the technicians developed the so-called Laser Range Finder (LRF).
He notices obstacles that are at a distance of up to two meters in front of the BR23C and are within a radius of 180 degrees, calculates the distance to the object and sends a signal to an on-board microprocessor, which is translated directly into signal for avoiding the collision
`At the moment when obstacle is noticed, the little robot will imitate the bee flight and immediately change direction by turning its wheels at 90o or even greater angles to avoid the collision.
The biggest difference from other arbitrary systems is that this evasive manoeuvre takes place absolutely instinctively.
If it weren’t so, the robot might not be able to respond with sufficient speed to avoid obstacles,” explains Toshiyuki Andoh, director of the Laboratory of Mobility and the lead engineer of this project.
In contrast to a bee, the robot cannot deviate upwards or down, but also not obscure; it can only move on two dimensions and in the direction that allows it to turn its wheels and not in the infinite directions and in infinite ways as the bees do.
For this reason, in addition to acceleration and deceleration functions, Nissan has used the rotation function so that the small robot avoids the collisions.
`This device only needs to process the information for a few seconds and act according to this information.
It does not require neither the information nor the intuitive logic of the maneuvers, so it can work continuously with only a few dozens of KB of storage space.
This sounds surprising, but it's not abound the GBs and it's not even about the MBs of space.
This is the first step in the development of technologies for the immediate prevention of collisions, a technology that Nissan prophesies to introduce to production cars over the next decade.
The robot works with lithium-polymer batteries, which are lighter and last longer as a variety of lithium-ion batteries.
Another possible application of this technology in the future could be the integration of the unit with a GPS navigation system by satellite to lead while driving to the destination while avoidinging the obstacles on the way.
